=== Parametric (mid-point conversion)
<parametric-mid-point-conversion>
Parametric methods can be employed when analyzing data, provided that the underlying assumptions of these methods are satisfied. For data that is based on ordinal scores, one way to apply parametric methods is to convert these scores into the mid-points of their corresponding percent scales. Once converted, the data is better suited for parametric analyses. Among the available techniques, the most frequently utilized is the application of an ANOVA (Analysis of Variance) model. This model is particularly useful for testing the null hypothesis, which posits that there are no significant differences among the treatments being studied.

We can use the #NormalTok("aov"); function to fit the model and check the parametric assumptions using the #NormalTok("DHARMa"); package.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("m1_mp ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("aov");#NormalTok("(midpoint ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" treatment, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" dat_ordinal_mp)");],
[#CommentTok("# normality and homoscedasticity tests");],
[#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(DHARMa)");],
[#FunctionTok("plot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("simulateResiduals");#NormalTok("(m1_mp))");],));
#figure([
#box(image("data-ordinal_files/figure-typst/fig-ordinal-resid-midpoint-1.svg"))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Residual diagnostics (DHARMa) of the analysis of variance fitted to the mid-point severity values
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-ordinal-resid-midpoint>


Since both normality and homoscedasticity assumptions are violated in our initial model, we will halt further analysis using this model. Instead, we will explore an alternative approach that includes data transformation (logit).

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# data transformation");],
[#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(car)");],
[#CommentTok("# transform midpoint to proportion");],
[#NormalTok("dat_ordinal_mp");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("midpoint2 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" dat_ordinal_mp");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("midpoint");#SpecialCharTok("/");#DecValTok("100");],
[],
[#NormalTok("m2_mp ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("aov");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("logit");#NormalTok("(midpoint2) ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" treatment, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" dat_ordinal_mp)");],
[#FunctionTok("plot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("simulateResiduals");#NormalTok("(m2_mp)) ");#CommentTok("# assumptions are met");],));
#figure([
#box(image("data-ordinal_files/figure-typst/fig-ordinal-resid-logit-1.svg"))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Residual diagnostics (DHARMa) of the analysis of variance fitted to the logit-transformed mid-point values
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-ordinal-resid-logit>


Now that both assumptions have been met, we can proceed to use a means comparison test to determine which treatments differ from one another, with the assistance of the {emmeans} package. It's important to note that the results are displayed in the original scale after transformation, specifically when using #NormalTok("type = \"response\"");.

