=== Parametric and non-parametric paired sample tests
<parametric-and-non-parametric-paired-sample-tests>
When two estimates are gathered from the same rater at different times, these data points are not independent. In such situations, a #strong[paired sample t-test] can be utilized to test if the mean difference between two sets of observations is zero. This test requires each subject (or leaf, in our context) to be measured or estimated twice, resulting in #emph[pairs] of observations. However, if the assumptions of the test (such as normality) are violated, a non-parametric equivalent, such as the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, also known as the #strong[Wilcoxon test], can be employed. This alternative is particularly useful when the data are not normally distributed.

To proceed with these tests, we first need to ascertain whether the paired differences are normally distributed. (The paired t-test does not require equal variances, because it is based on the differences.) Let's now apply the two tests to our data and compare the results.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# normality test of the paired differences");],
[#FunctionTok("shapiro.test");#NormalTok("(sbr");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("diff)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Shapiro-Wilk normality test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  sbr$diff");],
[#NormalTok("W = 0.80256, p-value = 0.0009437");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# paired t-test");],
[#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(sbr");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("aided, sbr");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("unaided, ");#AttributeTok("paired =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Paired t-test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  sbr$aided and sbr$unaided");],
[#NormalTok("t = 8.812, df = 19, p-value = 3.873e-08");],
[#NormalTok("alternative hypothesis: true mean difference is not equal to 0");],
[#NormalTok("95 percent confidence interval:");],
[#NormalTok(" 0.1216158 0.1973842");],
[#NormalTok("sample estimates:");],
[#NormalTok("mean difference ");],
[#NormalTok("         0.1595 ");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# Wilcoxon test");],
[#FunctionTok("wilcox.test");#NormalTok("(sbr");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("aided, sbr");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("unaided, ");#AttributeTok("paired =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in wilcox.test.default(sbr$aided, sbr$unaided, paired = TRUE): cannot");],
[#NormalTok("compute exact p-value with ties");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Wilcoxon signed rank test with continuity correction");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  sbr$aided and sbr$unaided");],
[#NormalTok("V = 210, p-value = 9.449e-05");],
[#NormalTok("alternative hypothesis: true location shift is not equal to 0");],));
]
]
The Shapiro-Wilk test indicates that the differences depart from normality (P \< 0.001), so the non-parametric test is the safer choice. Both tests nevertheless reject the null hypothesis of no difference between the aided and unaided conditions.

=== Mixed effects modeling
<mixed-effects-modeling>
Mixed models extend linear models to data in which the observations are not independent. Here each rater estimated severity twice, once without and once with the SAD, so the estimates of the same rater are correlated @brown2021. A mixed model has #emph[fixed effects], the regression parameters of interest (here, the effect of using the SAD), and #emph[random effects], which capture the variation among groups of observations (here, among raters).

We treat raters as a random effect because they are a sample from a larger population of possible raters, and we want conclusions that apply to that population. Accounting for the differences among raters also makes the comparison between the aided and unaided conditions more precise.

Let's start reshaping our data to the long format and assign them to a new data frame.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sbr2 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" sbr ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("pivot_longer");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("names_to =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"condition\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("values_to =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"estimate\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
]
Now we fit the mixed model using the #NormalTok("lmer"); function of the #emph[lme4] package. We will fit the model to the logit of the estimate because they should be bounded between zero and one. Preliminary analysis using non-transformed or log-transformed data resulted in lack of normality of residuals and heteroscedasticity (not shown).

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(lme4) ");],
[#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(car) ");#CommentTok("# for logit function");],
[#NormalTok("mix ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("logit");#NormalTok("(estimate) ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" condition ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" rater), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" sbr2)");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Check model performance");],
[#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(performance)");],
[#FunctionTok("check_normality");#NormalTok("(mix)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("OK: residuals appear as normally distributed (p = 0.381).");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("check_heteroscedasticity");#NormalTok("(mix)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("OK: Error variance appears to be homoscedastic (p = 0.961).");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# Check effect of condition");],
[#NormalTok("car");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("Anova");#NormalTok("(mix)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Analysis of Deviance Table (Type II Wald chisquare tests)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Response: logit(estimate)");],
[#NormalTok("           Chisq Df Pr(>Chisq)    ");],
[#NormalTok("condition 458.44  1  < 2.2e-16 ***");],
[#NormalTok("---");],
[#NormalTok("Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# Estimate the means for each group");],
[#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(emmeans)");],
[#NormalTok("em ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("emmeans");#NormalTok("(mix, ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" condition, ");#AttributeTok("type =");#StringTok("\"response\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("em");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok(" condition response      SE   df lower.CL upper.CL");],
[#NormalTok(" aided        0.968 0.00359 25.5    0.959    0.974");],
[#NormalTok(" unaided      0.817 0.01720 25.5    0.779    0.849");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Degrees-of-freedom method: kenward-roger ");],
[#NormalTok("Confidence level used: 0.95 ");],
[#NormalTok("Intervals are back-transformed from the logit scale ");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# Contrast the means");],
[#FunctionTok("pairs");#NormalTok("(em)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok(" contrast        odds.ratio    SE df null t.ratio p.value");],
[#NormalTok(" aided / unaided       6.72 0.597 19    1  21.411 <0.0001");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Degrees-of-freedom method: kenward-roger ");],
[#NormalTok("Tests are performed on the log odds ratio scale ");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# plot the means with 95% CIs");],
[#FunctionTok("plot");#NormalTok("(em) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("coord_flip");#NormalTok("()");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("xlim");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("0.7");#NormalTok(",");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_r4pde");#NormalTok("()");],));
#figure([
#box(image("data-sads_files/figure-typst/fig-sads-emm-1.svg"))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Estimated mean CCC and 95% confidence intervals for the unaided and aided conditions, from the mixed model fitted to the logit-transformed CCC
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-sads-emm>


As shown above, we can reject the null hypothesis that the means are the same between the two groups.

Alternatively, we can fit a generalized linear mixed model (GLMM), which allows the response to follow a distribution other than the normal. Because the CCC is bounded between 0 and 1, a beta distribution is a natural choice. The #NormalTok("glmmTMB()"); function of the #emph[glmmTMB] package fits GLMMs with a variety of distributions @brooks2017. We select the beta distribution with #NormalTok("family = beta_family()");.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(glmmTMB)");],
[#NormalTok("mix2 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("glmmTMB");#NormalTok("(estimate ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" condition ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" rater), ");],
[#NormalTok("                 ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" sbr2, ");],
[#NormalTok("                 ");#AttributeTok("family =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("beta_family");#NormalTok("())");],));
]
Residual diagnostics for GLMMs fitted with #NormalTok("glmmTMB()"); are best obtained with the #emph[DHARMa] package, which compares standardized residuals with residuals simulated from the fitted model.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(DHARMa)");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("plot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("simulateResiduals");#NormalTok("(mix2))");],));
#figure([
#box(image("data-sads_files/figure-typst/fig-sads-dharma-1.svg"))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Residual diagnostics (DHARMa) of the beta mixed model fitted with glmmTMB
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-sads-dharma>


The simulated residuals are uniformly distributed, which is what we expect when the model fits the data well. We can now proceed with the post hoc comparison, and we see that the results are similar to those obtained with the logit-transformed response.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("car");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("Anova");#NormalTok("(mix2)");],));
