==== Defining the model
<defining-the-model>
We will use a logistic regression which is a simple model. It may be useful to have a look at #link("https://mlu-explain.github.io/logistic-regression/")[this explanation] of such a model.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("spec_lr ");#OtherTok("<-");],
[#FunctionTok("logistic_reg");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("set_engine");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"glm\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("set_mode");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"classification\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
]
==== Defining the recipe
<defining-the-recipe>
The #NormalTok("recipe()"); function to be used here has two arguments:

- A formula. Any variable on the left-hand side of the tilde (\~) is considered the model outcome (here, outcome). On the right-hand side of the tilde are the predictors. Variables may be listed by name, or you can use the dot (.) to indicate all other variables as predictors.

- The data. A recipe is associated with the data set used to create the model. This will typically be the training set, so #NormalTok("data = data_train"); here.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("recipe_lr ");#OtherTok("<-");],
