===== Defining the model
<defining-the-model-1>
We will use a #link("https://uc-r.github.io/gbm_regression")[gradient boosting machine] algorithm which seems a robust algorithm for different applications.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("spec_lr ");#OtherTok("<-");],
[#FunctionTok("boost_tree");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("mode =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"classification\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("trees=");#DecValTok("1000");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#CommentTok("#tree_depth = tune::tune(),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#CommentTok("#learn_rate = tune::tune(),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#CommentTok("#loss_reduction = tune::tune()");],
[#NormalTok("  ) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("set_engine");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"xgboost\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("objective =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"multi:softprob\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("eval_metric =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"mlogloss\"");#NormalTok(",) ");],));
]
===== Defining the recipe
<defining-the-recipe-1>
The #NormalTok("recipe()"); function to be used here has two arguments:

- A formula. Any variable on the left-hand side of the tilde (\~) is considered the model outcome (here, outcome). On the right-hand side of the tilde are the predictors. Variables may be listed by name, or you can use the dot (.) to indicate all other variables as predictors.

- The data. A recipe is associated with the data set used to create the model. This will typically be the training set, so data = data\_train here.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("recipe_lr ");#OtherTok("<-");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("recipe");#NormalTok("(category ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ., data_train) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_role");#NormalTok("(Plot, ");#AttributeTok("new_role =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"id\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("step_zv");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("all_predictors");#NormalTok("()) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("step_corr");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("all_predictors");#NormalTok("())");],));
]
===== Model performance metrics
<model-performance-metrics>
Next, we need to specify what we would like to see for determining the performance of the model. Different modelling algorithms have different types of metrics. Because we have a multiclass classification problem (different categories of severity), we will choose the accuracy evaluation metric here.

===== Combine model specification and recipe into a workflow
<combine-model-specification-and-recipe-into-a-workflow>
We will want to use our recipe across several steps as we train and test our model. We will:

- Process the recipe using the training set: This involves any estimation or calculations based on the training set. For our recipe, the training set will be used to determine which predictors should be converted to dummy variables and which predictors will have zero-variance in the training set, and should be slated for removal.

- Apply the recipe to the training set: We create the final predictor set on the training set.

- Apply the recipe to the test set: We create the final predictor set on the test set. Nothing is recomputed and no information from the test set is used here; the dummy variable and zero-variance results from the training set are applied to the test set.

To simplify this process, we can use a model workflow, which pairs a model and recipe together. This is a straightforward approach because different recipes are often needed for different models, so when a model and recipe are bundled, it becomes easier to train and test workflows. We'll use the workflows package from tidymodels to bundle our model with our recipe.

Now we are ready to set up our complete modelling workflow. This workflow contains the model specification and the recipe.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("wf_cls_wilt ");#OtherTok("<-");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("workflow");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("spec =");#NormalTok(" spec_lr,");],
[#NormalTok("    recipe_lr");],
[#NormalTok("    )");],
[],
[#NormalTok("wf_cls_wilt");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("══ Workflow ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════");],
[#NormalTok("Preprocessor: Recipe");],
[#NormalTok("Model: boost_tree()");],
[],
[#NormalTok("── Preprocessor ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────");],
[#NormalTok("2 Recipe Steps");],
[],
[#NormalTok("• step_zv()");],
[#NormalTok("• step_corr()");],
[],
[#NormalTok("── Model ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────");],
[#NormalTok("Boosted Tree Model Specification (classification)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Main Arguments:");],
[#NormalTok("  trees = 1000");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Engine-Specific Arguments:");],
[#NormalTok("  objective = multi:softprob");],
[#NormalTok("  eval_metric = mlogloss");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Computational engine: xgboost ");],));
]
]
