== How epidemics occur
<how-epidemics-occur>
Before knowing how epidemics develop in time, it is important to understand how an epidemic occurs. An epidemic begins when the #strong[primary inoculum] (a variable number of propagules able to infect the plant) that is #emph[surviving] somewhere establishes an intimate contact with individuals of the host population - this process is called #emph[infection]. These inocula are usually surviving externally to the plant host and need to #emph[disperse] (move), passively or by means of a vector, to reach the plant. It can also be that a growing host encounters a localized (static) source of inoculum.

Once the infection is established, the pathogen #emph[colonizes] the plant tissues and disease symptoms are noticed. When this happens, the #strong[incubation period] can be measured in time units. A successful colonization will lead to #emph[reproduction] of the pathogen inside and/or external to the crop, and so the #strong[latent period] is completed, and can also be measured in time units. Finally, the #strong[infectious period] takes place and continues until the pathogen is no longer capable of producing the #strong[secondary inoculum] on the infected site.

#figure([
#box(image("imgs/disease_cycle.png", width: 5.10417in))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Five main processes of the disease cycle
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-diagram>


Epidemiologists are generally interested in determining the length of the incubation, latent, and infectious periods as influenced by factors related to the host, pathogen, or environment. This is relevant because the longer it takes for the completion of the incubation and latent periods, the lower the potential number of repeated cycles. In summary, a single "infection cycle" represents all events that occur from infection to dispersal, and this occurs only once for many diseases, while for others there may be multiple cycles, which are defined as an "infection chain."

