Two of the three curves show a sigmoid shape with the exception of group 3 that resembles an exponential growth, not reaching the maximum value, and thus suggesting an incomplete epidemic. We can easily eliminate the monomolecular and exponential models and decide on the other two non-flexible models: logistic or Gompertz. To do that, let's proceed to model fitting and evaluate the statistics for supporting a final decision. There are two modeling approaches for model fitting in epifitter: the #strong[linear] or #strong[nonlinear] parameter-estimation methods.

=== epifitter: linear regression
<epifitter-linear-regression>
Among the several options offered by #emph[epifitter] we start with the simplest one, which is to fit a model to a single epidemic using the linear regression approach. For such, the #NormalTok("fit_lin()"); requires two arguments: time (#NormalTok("time");) and disease intensity (#NormalTok("y");) each one as a vector stored or not in a dataframe.

Since we have three epidemics, #NormalTok("fit_lin()"); will be used three times. The function produces a list object with six elements. Let's first look at the #NormalTok("Stats"); dataframe of each of the three lists named #NormalTok("epi1"); to #NormalTok("epi3");.

