For multiple epidemics, we can use another handy function that allows us to simultaneously fit the models to multiple DPC data. Different from #NormalTok("fit_lin()");, #NormalTok("fit_multi()"); requires the data to be structured in the long format where there is a column specifying each of the epidemics.

Let's then create a new data set called #NormalTok("pepper2"); using the data transposing functions of the #emph[tidyr] package.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("pepper2 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" pepper ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("pivot_longer");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("names_to =");#StringTok("\"treat\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("values_to =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"inc\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
]
Now we fit the models to all DPCs. Note that the name of the variable indicating the DPC code needs to be specified in the #NormalTok("strata_cols"); argument. To use the nonlinear regression approach we set #NormalTok("nlin"); argument to #NormalTok("TRUE");.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("epi_all ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fit_multi");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("time_col =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"t\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("intensity_col =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"inc\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" pepper2,");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("strata_cols =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"treat\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("nlin =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("FALSE");],
[#NormalTok(")");],));
]
Now let's select the statistics of model fitting. Again, #emph[Epifitter] ranks the models based on the CCC (the higher the better) but it is important to check the RSE as well - the lower the better. In fact, the RSE is more important when the goal is prediction.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("epi_all");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("Parameters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(treat, model, best_CCC, best_RSE, RSE, CCC)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("   treat         model best_CCC best_RSE       RSE       CCC");],
[#NormalTok("1      1   Exponential        4        3 0.6705085 0.7839381");],
[#NormalTok("2      1      Gompertz        1        2 0.5911056 0.9847857");],
[#NormalTok("3      1      Logistic        3        4 0.8235798 0.9781534");],
[#NormalTok("4      1 Monomolecular        2        1 0.5431977 0.9838044");],
[#NormalTok("5      2   Exponential        4        4 1.2015809 0.8971003");],
[#NormalTok("6      2      Gompertz        2        2 0.8407922 0.9707204");],
[#NormalTok("7      2      Logistic        1        1 0.4523616 0.9961683");],
[#NormalTok("8      2 Monomolecular        3        3 1.0683633 0.9247793");],
[#NormalTok("9      3   Exponential        3        4 0.7705736 0.9635747");],
[#NormalTok("10     3      Gompertz        2        1 0.2262550 0.9824935");],
[#NormalTok("11     3      Logistic        1        3 0.6045243 0.9829434");],
[#NormalTok("12     3 Monomolecular        4        2 0.2533763 0.8591837");],));
]
]
The code below calculates the frequency that each model was the best. This would facilitate the analysis in the case of many epidemics.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("freq_best ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" epi_all");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("Parameters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(best_CCC ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#FunctionTok("group_by");#NormalTok("(treat, model) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("first =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("n");#NormalTok("()) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("ungroup");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("count");#NormalTok("(model) ");],
[#NormalTok("freq_best ");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 2 × 2");],
[#NormalTok("  model        n");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>    <int>");],
[#NormalTok("1 Gompertz     1");],
[#NormalTok("2 Logistic     2");],));
]
]
We can see that the Logistic model was the best model in two out of three epidemics.

To be more certain about our decision, let's advance to the final step which is to produce the plots with the observed and predicted values for each assessment time by calling the #NormalTok("Data"); dataframe of the #NormalTok("epi_all"); list.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("epi_all");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("Data ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(model ");#SpecialCharTok("%in%");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Gompertz\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Logistic\"");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(time, predicted, ");#AttributeTok("shape =");#NormalTok(" treat)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
