We can compare the rate parameter (slopes) from two separate linear regression models using a t-test. This is sometimes referred to as a "test of parallelism" in the context of comparing slopes. The t-statistic for comparing two slopes with their respective standard errors can be calculated as:

$t = frac(beta_1 - beta_2, sqrt(S E_(beta_1)^2 + S E_(beta_2)^2))$

This t-statistic follows a t-distribution with $d f = n_1 + n_2 - 4$ degrees of freedom, where $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes of the two groups. In our case, $n_1 = n_2 = 8$, so $d f = 8 + 8 - 4 = 12$.

Here's how to perform the t-test for comparing curve 1 and 2.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("# Given slopes and standard errors from curve 1 and 2");],
[#NormalTok("beta1 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.2104");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("beta2 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.2784");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("SE_beta1 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.01815");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("SE_beta2 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.00997");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Sample sizes for both treatments (n1 and n2)");],
[#NormalTok("n1 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("8");],
[#NormalTok("n2 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("8");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Calculate the t-statistic");],
[#NormalTok("t_statistic ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("abs");#NormalTok("(beta1 ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" beta2) ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sqrt");#NormalTok("(SE_beta1");#SpecialCharTok("^");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" SE_beta2");#SpecialCharTok("^");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Degrees of freedom");],
[#NormalTok("df ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" n1 ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" n2 ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Calculate the p-value");],
[#NormalTok("p_value ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("pt");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("abs");#NormalTok("(t_statistic), df))");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# Print the results");],
[#FunctionTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("paste");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"t-statistic:\"");#NormalTok(", ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(t_statistic, ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] \"t-statistic: 3.2837\"");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("paste");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Degrees of freedom:\"");#NormalTok(", df))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] \"Degrees of freedom: 12\"");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("paste");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"p-value:\"");#NormalTok(", ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(p_value, ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] \"p-value: 0.0065\"");],));
]
]
The #NormalTok("pt()"); function in R gives the cumulative distribution function of the t-distribution. The #NormalTok("2 * (1 - pt(abs(t_statistic), df))"); line calculates the two-tailed p-value. This will tell us if the slopes are significantly different at the chosen alpha level (commonly 0.05).

== Designed experiments
<designed-experiments>
In the following section, we'll focus on disease data collected over time from the same plot unit, also known as repeated measures. These data come from a designed experiment aimed at evaluating and comparing the effects of different treatments.

Specifically, we'll use a dataset of progress curves found on page 98 of "Study of Plant Disease Epidemics" @chapter2007b. These curves depict the incidence of soybean plants showing symptoms of bud blight, which is caused by the tobacco streak virus. Four different treatments, corresponding to different planting dates, were evaluated using a randomized complete block design with four replicates. Each curve has four time-based assessments.

The data for this study is stored in a CSV file, which we'll load into our environment using the read\_csv() function. Once loaded, we'll store the data in a dataframe named budblight.

