In both cases, approximately 94% of all hosts are infected by the end of the epidemic, with a very small discrepancy between the two calculations because the epidemic has not quite completed within $T_(m a x) = 250$ days.

=== Initial rate of epidemic increase
<initial-rate-of-epidemic-increase>
A complementary question concerns the speed of epidemic growth during the early stages of spread.

When disease is rare, almost all hosts are healthy, so the epidemic is operating near the disease-free equilibrium. In this regime, the nonlinear model behaves approximately like a linear system. The dominant behaviour of that linear system determines whether infection grows or declines, and how rapidly this occurs. The quantity $r$ is the corresponding exponential growth rate.

This initial exponential rate of disease increase can be found @Cunniffe2012 by calculating the largest real part of the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix of the system evaluated at the disease-free-equilibrium $ \( H \, L \, I \, R \) = \( N \, 0 \, 0 \, 0 \) $ The Jacobian matrix is simply a mathematical object that captures how small perturbations to each compartment affect the rates of change of all other compartments.

This can be done using the function #NormalTok("jacobian.full()"); in the #NormalTok("rootSolve"); package.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(rootSolve)");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# disease free equilibrium");],
[#NormalTok("hlir.dfe ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("H =");#NormalTok(" N, ");],
[#NormalTok("              ");#AttributeTok("L =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");],
[#NormalTok("              ");#AttributeTok("I =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");],
[#NormalTok("              ");#AttributeTok("R =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# find jacobian matrix at hlir.dfe");],
[#NormalTok("hlir.jac ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("jacobian.full");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" hlir.dfe, ");],
[#NormalTok("                          ");#AttributeTok("func =");#NormalTok(" hlir.model,");],
[#NormalTok("                          ");#AttributeTok("parms =");#NormalTok(" hlir.params)");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# find eigenvalues and eigenvectors");],
[#NormalTok("hlir.eigen.jac ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("eigen");#NormalTok("(hlir.jac)");],
[],
[#CommentTok("# extract and print the largest eigenvalue");],
[#FunctionTok("print");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("paste");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"r =\"");#NormalTok(",");#FunctionTok("max");#NormalTok("(hlir.eigen.jac[[");#StringTok("\"values\"");#NormalTok("]])))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] \"r = 0.1\"");],));
]
]
Note that the calculation done by the code above involving the function #NormalTok("rootSolve::jacobian.full()"); would in fact be the same for any compartmental model, and so does not require any mathematics. You do not need to understand the linear algebra here. The software does the calculation automatically.

That the numerical solution matches these theoretical expectations can be checked.

