The values we obtained here are not the same but quite close to the values reported in #cite(<madden2007>, form: "prose"). For the transect data, the calculated coefficients in the book example for lags 1, 2 and 3 are 0.625, 0.144, and - 0.041. The conclusion is the same, the smaller the distance between sampling units, the stronger is the correlation between the count values.

===== Moran's I
<morans-i>
The autocorrelation coefficient above is closely related to Moran's I @Moran1950, a widely used statistic of spatial autocorrelation. Positive autocorrelation means that nearby locations have more similar values than expected under a random arrangement, and negative autocorrelation means that they have more dissimilar values.

Let's use another example dataset from the book to calculate the Moran's I in R. The data is shown in page 269 of the book. The data represent the number of diseased plants per quadrat (out of a total of 100 plants in each) in 144 quadrats. It was based on an epidemic generated using the stochastic simulator of #cite(<xu2004>, form: "prose"). The data is stored in a CSV file.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("epi ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("read_csv");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/emdelponte/epidemiology-R/main/data/xu-madden-simulated.csv\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("epi1 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" epi ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("pivot_longer");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("13");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("               ");#AttributeTok("names_to =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"y\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("               ");#AttributeTok("values_to =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"n\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("pull");#NormalTok("(n)");],));
]
The {spdep} package provides the function #NormalTok("moran()"); to calculate Moran's I. It needs two inputs: a numeric vector with the values (here, the number of diseased plants in each quadrat) and a spatial weights list that defines which quadrats are neighbors. Let's load the library and create the weights.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("100");#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(spdep)");],));
]
The #NormalTok("cell2nb()"); function creates the neighbor list with 12 rows and 12 columns, which is how the 144 quadrats are arranged.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("nb ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("cell2nb");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("type =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"queen\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("torus =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("FALSE");#NormalTok(")");],));
]
The #NormalTok("nb2listw()"); function supplements a neighbors list with spatial weights for the chosen coding scheme. We use the default W, which is the row standardized (sums over all links to n). We then create the #NormalTok("col.W"); neighbor list.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("col.W ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("nb2listw");#NormalTok("(nb, ");#AttributeTok("style =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"W\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
]
The Moran's I statistic is given by the #NormalTok("moran()"); function

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("moran");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" epi1, ");#CommentTok("# numeric vector");],
[#NormalTok("      ");#AttributeTok("listw =");#NormalTok(" col.W, ");#CommentTok("# the nb list");],
[#NormalTok("      ");#AttributeTok("n =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("length");#NormalTok("(epi1), ");#CommentTok("# number of zones");],
[#NormalTok("      ");#AttributeTok("S0 =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("Szero");#NormalTok("(col.W)) ");#CommentTok("# global sum of weights");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("$I");],
[#NormalTok("[1] 0.6982314");],
[],
[#NormalTok("$K");],
[#NormalTok("[1] 2.878088");],));
]
]
#NormalTok("$I"); is Moran's I and #NormalTok("$K"); is the sample kurtosis of the values.

A positive Moran's I indicates positive spatial autocorrelation (nearby quadrats have similar values), a negative value indicates negative autocorrelation (neighboring quadrats tend to be dissimilar), and a value close to zero indicates no spatial autocorrelation.

===== Moran's test
<morans-test>
To test whether the autocorrelation is statistically significant, we use #NormalTok("moran.test()"); of the {spdep} package. Its two main inputs are #NormalTok("x");, the numeric vector of values, and #NormalTok("listw");, the spatial weights list created above.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("moran.test");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" epi1, ");],
[#NormalTok("           ");#AttributeTok("listw =");#NormalTok(" col.W)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Moran I test under randomisation");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  epi1  ");],
[#NormalTok("weights: col.W    ");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Moran I statistic standard deviate = 15.919, p-value < 2.2e-16");],
[#NormalTok("alternative hypothesis: greater");],
[#NormalTok("sample estimates:");],
[#NormalTok("Moran I statistic       Expectation          Variance ");],
[#NormalTok("      0.698231416      -0.006993007       0.001962596 ");],));
]
]
The function returns Moran's I, its expected value under the null hypothesis of no spatial autocorrelation, its variance and a P-value. A significant positive value indicates that similar values cluster together, a significant negative value indicates that dissimilar values are neighbors, and a non-significant value gives no evidence of spatial autocorrelation.

As before, we can construct a correlogram using the output of the #NormalTok("sp.correlogram()"); function. Note that the figure below is very similar to the one shown in Figure 91.5 in page 269 of the book chapter @chapter2007. Let's store the results in a data frame.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("correl_I ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sp.correlogram");#NormalTok("(nb, epi1, ");],
[#NormalTok("                           ");#AttributeTok("order =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("                           ");#AttributeTok("method =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"I\"");#NormalTok(",  ");],
[#NormalTok("                           ");#AttributeTok("zero.policy =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in nblag(neighbours, maxlag = order): lag 7 neighbour object has 5");],
[#NormalTok("sub-graphs");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in nblag(neighbours, maxlag = order): lag 8 neighbour object has 17");],
[#NormalTok("sub-graphs");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in nblag(neighbours, maxlag = order): lag 9 neighbour object has 37");],
[#NormalTok("sub-graphs");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in nblag(neighbours, maxlag = order): lag 10 neighbour object has 65");],
[#NormalTok("sub-graphs");],));
]
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("df_correl ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("data.frame");#NormalTok("(correl_I");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("res) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("lag =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok("))");],
[#CommentTok("# Show the spatial autocorrelation for 10 distance lags");],
[#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(df_correl");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("X1,");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok(" [1]  0.698  0.340  0.086 -0.002 -0.009 -0.024 -0.090 -0.180 -0.217 -0.124");],));
