We can then create a function named #NormalTok("calculate_TRH9010()");. This function calculates the number of hours when specified condition is met (i.e., hours when 15 \<= T \<= 30 & RH \> 90), the probability (#emph[p]) of an epidemic, and the classification of the epidemic (#emph[epi]) as 0 or 1 based on a defined threshold for p (0.36 in this case) @dewolf2003. The function's arguments are the weather data frame, YEAR, MO, and DY, allowing us to estimate the risk for a specific day (the flowering date) within the downloaded period. It's important to note that, in the original model, the variables were normalized (its value divided by the maximum observed value). Thus, the hour count for TRH9010 should be divided by the constant 136.

